        concept: "Java's core language rules are deceptively simple to state but genuinely easy to get subtly wrong under interview pressure - this is precisely why Knowvation's screening round tests them directly rather than assuming familiarity.\n\nMETHOD OVERLOADING (same method name, different parameter list, resolved at COMPILE time based on the declared argument types) is fundamentally different from METHOD OVERRIDING (a subclass redefining a superclass method with the IDENTICAL signature, resolved at RUNTIME based on the object's actual type) - this distinction alone is one of the single most commonly tested Java concepts in screening rounds, precisely because confusing them reveals a genuine gap in understanding polymorphism, not just a vocabulary slip.\n\nJava is genuinely, strictly PASS-BY-VALUE - always, with zero exceptions, even for objects: what actually gets passed into a method is a COPY of the reference (the memory address), not the object itself and not the original reference variable. This is precisely why reassigning a parameter INSIDE a method never affects the caller's own variable, while MUTATING the object the reference points to (calling a setter, modifying a list) genuinely does affect the caller's object - both facts are true simultaneously, and conflating them is a classic beginner confusion point interviewers specifically probe for.",
