        concept: "The four pillars of Object-Oriented Programming - Encapsulation, Abstraction, Inheritance, Polymorphism - are frequently recited as buzzwords, but Knowvation's interviewers specifically probe whether you understand WHY each one is genuinely useful, not just that it exists.\n\nENCAPSULATION (bundling data with the methods that operate on it, hiding internal state behind private fields and public getters/setters - think of a medicine capsule: the active ingredients are hidden inside, you interact only through the intended interface) protects an object's internal consistency - a bank account class controlling deposits/withdrawals through methods (never letting external code directly set the balance field) can enforce \"never go negative\" as an invariant that direct field access could never guarantee.\n\nABSTRACTION is genuinely related but distinct: it's about hiding COMPLEXITY, not just data - exposing a simple, high-level interface (a car's steering wheel and pedals) while hiding the messy implementation details (the engine, transmission) entirely. Encapsulation is the MECHANISM (private fields, access modifiers); abstraction is the DESIGN GOAL (a simpler mental model for the user of your class) - encapsulation is how you typically ACHIEVE abstraction in practice.\n\nINTERFACE versus ABSTRACT CLASS is a genuinely practical design decision, not just syntax trivia: an interface defines a pure CONTRACT (what a class can DO, with no shared implementation state before Java 8's default methods) - use it when unrelated classes need to guarantee the same capability (Comparable, Runnable). An abstract class can hold real shared STATE and partial implementation - use it when subclasses genuinely share a common base identity and some common code, not just a capability.",
