        concept: "Output-prediction questions test something genuinely different from \"can you write code\" - they test whether you can accurately SIMULATE a program's execution in your head, step by step, without a compiler to catch mistakes for you.\n\nThe single most reliable technique: for any loop, explicitly write out the value of every tracked variable at the END of each iteration, rather than trying to jump straight to the final answer - most errors in these questions come from an OFF-BY-ONE mistake (does the loop run n or n+1 times? does it include or exclude the boundary?) that only shows up when you trace iteration-by-iteration rather than guessing.\n\nINTEGER DIVISION is a classic, specific trap: in Java (and most C-family languages), dividing two INTEGERS truncates toward zero (7/2 evaluates to 3, not 3.5) - this becomes especially tricky combined with operator precedence, where an expression like `5 + 3 / 2` evaluates the division FIRST (division has higher precedence than addition), giving `5 + 1 = 6`, not `4` or `4.0`.\n\nBIG-O COMPLEXITY for simple loops follows directly from counting how many times the innermost operation actually executes: a single loop from 0 to n is O(n); a loop nested inside another loop, both running to n, is O(n^2) - REGARDLESS of what the innermost operation actually does, as long as it's O(1) itself; this exact counting logic is precisely what most complexity-analysis MCQs are testing.",
