        concept: "This category tests CONCEPTUAL understanding of core data structures (not implementing them from scratch, which DeVert's own dedicated DSA practice module covers in far greater depth) - specifically, whether you know WHICH structure fits WHICH problem shape.\n\nSTACK (Last-In-First-Out) is the right tool whenever a problem has a genuine \"most recent thing first\" access pattern: matching balanced parentheses/brackets, undo functionality, and function call tracking (the actual call stack) all share this exact LIFO shape. QUEUE (First-In-First-Out) fits the opposite pattern: processing tasks in the order they arrived - print queues, request handling, breadth-first traversal.\n\nTREE TRAVERSAL orders each serve a genuinely different practical purpose, worth knowing precisely: IN-ORDER traversal of a Binary SEARCH Tree specifically visits nodes in SORTED order (a direct, useful consequence of a BST's own left-smaller/right-larger structure) - this is precisely why in-order traversal is the standard way to extract a BST's contents in sorted order. PRE-ORDER traversal (visit the node itself before its children) is useful for COPYING/serializing a tree's structure, since you can reconstruct the tree from a pre-order sequence directly. POST-ORDER (visit children before the node itself) is used when children must genuinely be processed/deleted BEFORE the parent - like safely deleting an entire tree structure bottom-up.",
        keyPoints: ["Stack (LIFO) fits \"most recent first\" problems: balanced brackets, undo, call stacks; Queue (FIFO) fits \"arrival order\" problems: task processing, BFS", "In-order traversal of a BST visits nodes in sorted order - a direct, useful consequence of the BST's own structure", "Pre-order traversal suits copying/serializing a tree; post-order suits safely processing/deleting children before their parent"],
        commonMistakes: ["Reaching for a stack when a problem's actual access pattern is FIFO (arrival order), or vice versa - genuinely misidentifying the problem shape", "Confusing which traversal order gives sorted output for a BST - it's specifically in-order, not pre-order or post-order"],
        interviewTips: ["Be ready to explain, with a concrete example, why a specific problem (like validating balanced parentheses) genuinely needs a stack rather than a queue, and to state which BST traversal order gives sorted output"],
        questions: [
          q("Which data structure is the natural fit for checking whether parentheses in an expression are balanced?", ["Queue", "Stack", "Array", "HashMap"], 1, "Easy", "A stack's LIFO order naturally matches the \"most recently opened bracket must close first\" requirement.", ["DSA", "Stack"]),
          q("Which traversal order visits a Binary Search Tree's nodes in sorted order?", ["Pre-order", "Post-order", "In-order", "Level-order"], 2, "Medium", "In-order traversal (left, node, right) directly produces sorted output for a BST due to its own left-smaller/right-larger structure.", ["DSA", "Binary Search Tree", "Traversal"]),
          q("What underlying data structure does Breadth-First Search (BFS) typically use to track nodes to visit next?", ["Stack", "Queue", "Priority Heap only", "None - BFS is purely recursive"], 1, "Medium", "BFS processes nodes in the order they were discovered (FIFO), which is exactly what a queue provides.", ["DSA", "BFS", "Queue"]),
          q("What is the time complexity of searching for an element in a balanced Binary Search Tree?", ["O(n)", "O(log n)", "O(1)", "O(n^2)"], 1, "Medium", "A balanced BST halves the remaining search space at each level, giving O(log n).", ["DSA", "Binary Search Tree"]),
          q("Which traversal order is most useful for safely deleting an entire tree, freeing children before their parent?", ["Pre-order", "In-order", "Post-order", "Level-order"], 2, "Medium", "Post-order visits children before the parent node, matching the requirement to delete children first.", ["DSA", "Traversal"]),
          q("What is the defining property of a stack's access order?", ["First-In-First-Out (FIFO)", "Last-In-First-Out (LIFO)", "Random access", "Sorted order"], 1, "Easy", "A stack is LIFO - the most recently added element is the first one removed.", ["DSA", "Stack"]),
          q("What does Depth-First Search (DFS) typically use to track its traversal path?", ["A queue", "A stack (explicitly, or implicitly via recursion's own call stack)", "A hash table exclusively", "No auxiliary structure at all"], 1, "Medium", "DFS goes as deep as possible before backtracking - a stack (or recursion, which uses the call stack) matches this pattern directly.", ["DSA", "DFS", "Stack"]),
          q("What is a genuine real-world use case for a priority queue?", ["Storing elements in strict insertion order", "Processing tasks by priority level rather than arrival order (e.g., an OS scheduler, a hospital triage system)", "Guaranteeing constant-time random access by index", "Replacing a HashMap entirely"], 1, "Medium", "A priority queue always serves the highest (or lowest) priority element next, regardless of insertion order.", ["DSA", "Priority Queue"]),
        ],
