        name: "React Fundamentals",
        whatYoullLearn: ["What the virtual DOM actually is and why it makes React's rendering efficient", "The real difference between state and props, and when to use each", "How useEffect's dependency array genuinely controls when it runs"],
        concept: "React's VIRTUAL DOM is a lightweight, in-memory JavaScript representation of the actual browser DOM - when state changes, React builds a NEW virtual DOM tree, DIFFS it against the previous one (a process called reconciliation), and updates ONLY the actual, real DOM nodes that genuinely changed - this is precisely why React avoids the genuinely expensive cost of re-rendering the entire real DOM on every single state change.\n\nSTATE versus PROPS is a foundational, frequently-tested distinction: STATE is data a component OWNS and manages internally (via useState), which can change over that component's own lifetime and triggers a re-render when it does. PROPS are data PASSED DOWN from a parent component - read-only from the receiving child's own perspective (a child must never directly mutate its own props) - this one-directional \"props down, events up\" data flow is precisely React's core architectural principle.\n\nuseEffect's DEPENDENCY ARRAY genuinely controls exactly when the effect re-runs, worth knowing precisely: an EMPTY array `[]` means the effect runs ONCE, only after the initial render (component mount) - useful for one-time setup like a data fetch. NO array at all means the effect runs after EVERY single render - genuinely rarely what you actually want, and a common source of accidental infinite loops if the effect itself triggers a state update. An array WITH specific values means the effect re-runs only when one of THOSE specific values has genuinely changed since the last render - this precise mechanism is exactly what lets a component \"react\" to specific prop/state changes without needlessly re-running on every unrelated re-render.",
        keyPoints: ["The virtual DOM lets React diff a new render against the previous one and update only the actual DOM nodes that genuinely changed, avoiding expensive full-DOM re-renders", "State is data a component owns internally (changes trigger re-render); props are read-only data passed down from a parent - \"props down, events up\" is React's core data flow principle", "useEffect's dependency array precisely controls re-run timing: [] runs once on mount, no array runs every render, [specificValue] re-runs only when that value actually changes"],
